 "narrative": "The new validation evidence largely confirms the core dependency-score architecture but does not promote PTEN loss to a robust main association.\n\n1. TP53-loss main effect. The main positive-direction TP53-loss claim H13 remains accepted and can be closed as independently validated. Discovery showed a signed estimate of +0.423 with lower bound +0.327; independent validation showed +0.355 with a multiplicity-adjusted lower bound of +0.221. Both intervals lie above the 0.15 composite-unit threshold in the favorable direction. Because more negative dependency_composite means stronger apparent dependency, this means TP53-loss samples have higher, less negative composite scores in the supplied dataset. This is an association, not a causal claim.\n\n2. KEAP1-loss main effect. The validated KEAP1-loss positive effect remains the strongest supported main predictor: discovery +0.832 lower +0.766; validation +0.769 lower +0.676. The direction is stable and far above threshold. Interpretation: KEAP1-loss samples have higher, less negative composite dependency scores, i.e. weaker apparent composite dependency, in this dataset.\n\n3. SMARCA4-loss main effect. The SMARCA4-loss negative-direction claim remains accepted and independently validated: signed negative-direction estimate +0.817 validation lower +0.715, with discovery +0.833 lower +0.767. In raw scale, SMARCA4-loss samples have lower, more negative scores, i.e. stronger apparent composite dependency. The positive-direction counterpart remains rejected.\n\n4. Conditional architecture. The co-alteration analyses are broadly consistent. TP53 and KEAP1 effects persist in each other's co-mutation contexts, and both remain strong among SMARCA4-loss-free cells. SMARCA4-loss negative effects persist across TP53, KEAP1, adenocarcinoma, Avana, and Cas9-quality contexts. The TP53-by-KEAP1 interaction remains direction-supported but not threshold-decisive; it should be treated as weak uncertainty, not a validated interaction.\n\n5. H16 conditional TP53 effect among KEAP1-loss-free cells. Discovery was clearly above threshold: +0.367 lower +0.266. The independent validation estimate is +0.287, but the adjusted lower bound is +0.131, slightly below the 0.15 threshold. I therefore retain H16 as an accepted conditional association based primarily on the supplied discovery evidence, but I do not classify it as robustly independently validated. Investigation is deferred because additional subset analysis would not materially improve the main architecture.\n\n6. PTEN-loss overall main effect. The requested validation of PTEN-loss negative main effect H63 did not support promotion to a robust fourth main association. The discovery signed estimate was +0.227 with lower bound +0.131, and the independent validation signed estimate was +0.226 with lower bound +0.087. Both point estimates are directionally supportive and above the threshold, but neither independent interval excludes sub-threshold effects at the required level. I keep H63 unresolved and close the main PTEN-loss overall investigation. The best scientific interpretation is that PTEN loss is a near-threshold, context-limited signal in the supplied dataset, not a robust overall meaningful difference.\n\n7. PTEN conditional findings. The accepted PTEN-loss negative conditionals in TP53-loss-free cells (H80: +0.259 lower +0.169) and KEAP1-loss cells (H81: +0.378 lower +0.193) remain discovery-supported context findings. Their overall main-effect validation failure means these should not be described as generalized effects. The most informative PTEN follow-up is now either independent validation of the strongest existing context, preferably H80, or the proposed concentrated conditional test of PTEN loss among TP53-loss-free and KEAP1-loss cells, rather than additional broad subset mining.\n\nNegative findings include the binary research-marker claims and several driver screens, including BRCA2-loss positive and SMAD4-loss positive/negative directions. CDKN2A-loss negative and RB1-loss positive are near-threshold but not securely meaningful. Constructed research-signature interactions remain dataset-specific heterogeneity findings, not biological mechanism claims. Overall, the supported main associations are TP53-loss positive, KEAP1-loss positive, and SMARCA4-loss negative, with noncausal and dataset-specific interpretation. The main unresolved issue is whether any PTEN-loss subgroup can be validated above the threshold; otherwise PTEN should be reported as directionally suggestive but not established.",
 "research_notes": "Updated synthesis after iteration 5 validation response:\n\nCore supported and independently validated supplied-dataset associations:\n- TP53-loss positive main effect: accepted, closed, robust. Discovery signed +0.423 lower +0.327; validation signed +0.355 lower +0.221. Higher/less negative composite score in TP53-loss cells.\n- KEAP1-loss positive main effect: accepted, closed, strongest main predictor. Discovery +0.832 lower +0.766; validation +0.769 lower +0.676. Higher/less negative score in KEAP1-loss cells.\n- SMARCA4-loss negative main effect: accepted, closed, robust. Signed negative-direction discovery +0.833 lower +0.767; validation +0.817 lower +0.715. Lower/more negative score in SMARCA4-loss cells.\n\nConditional and context status:\n- TP53 positive persists in KEAP1-loss-free, KEAP1-loss, SMARCA4-loss-free, Avana, adenocarcinoma, and batch contexts. H16 validation is point-supportive but lower bound +0.131, below threshold, so it is accepted discovery-supported conditional but not independently threshold-validated. Investigation deferred.\n- KEAP1 positive persists broadly, including SMARCA4-loss-free; accepted conditionals remain strong.\n- SMARCA4 negative persists broadly across TP53/KEAP1, subtype, library, and Cas9 contexts.\n- TP53-by-KEAP1 interaction H65/H20 remains unresolved; lower bound near zero, so do not describe as validated interaction.\n\nPTEN-loss architecture:\n- Main negative-direction overall H63 is NOT promoted. Discovery signed +0.227 lower +0.131; validation signed +0.226 lower +0.087. Direction replicated but not threshold-secure. Main H63 kept unresolved and closed; report as near-threshold/context-limited signal.\n- Accepted discovery conditionals remain: TP53-loss-free cells H80 +0.259 lower +0.169; KEAP1-loss cells H81 +0.378 lower +0.193. These are not independently validated main effects.\n- Avana PTEN context H82 is directionally strong but lower +0.146, below threshold. Other PTEN subsets are weaker or non-decisive.\n- Registered refinement proposal: PTEN-loss negative among TP53-loss-free and KEAP1-loss cells, motivated by H80/H81. If this passes threshold, consider validation; otherwise stop PTEN subset expansion.\n\nNull/near-null findings:\n- Research markers D/E/F positive claims rejected.\n- BRCA2-loss positive rejected; SMAD4-loss positive/negative rejected; SMAD4 overall effect is not meaningful at threshold.\n- CDKN2A-loss negative remains near-threshold/unresolved; not accepted.\n- RB1-loss positive is near-threshold but not accepted.\n- Many genomic driver claims are still untested and should be prioritized for broad screening.\n\nNext plan:\n1. Continue broad screening of untested registered main driver claims: NF1 loss, APC mutation, PIK3CA mutation, BRAF V600E, EGFR amplification, ERBB2 amplification, MSI-high.\n2. For PTEN, pursue only one focused follow-up: proposed TP53-loss-free + KEAP1-loss conditional, and/or future independent validation of H80 if strong. Do not expand many PTEN subsets unless they produce threshold-crossing evidence.\n3. Treat signature interactions and marker interactions as constructed-assay context findings, not biological interpretations.\n4. All claims remain observational associations within the supplied dataset, not causal effects or generalized biological claims."
}
